Wes Morgan raises an interesting philosophical issue through his remarks. To
avoid flames, assume the following is a general set of comments, not
directed to any individual or his behavior.

The issue is (and one can see how it generalizes) whether a site may post a
warning message in such a way that it must be read, saying that the files in
a particular directory are not to be downloaded without permission, or
whether they must physically make it impossible, if the person at the other
end asserts he is an "honest man."

I'm not speaking about preventing deliberate criminal activity, here, but
the notion of what's necessary with honest people.

In other words, where does the ethical onus lie, if one asserts he is an
honest man and a site has given him notice, but not physically prevented him
from downloading?

To start the ball rolling, I assert that giving notice is all that is
required--that is, if notice has been given then it is the downloader who
has behaved in an ethically unacceptable way, and not the site just because
they didn't physically prevent him.

