In article <1993Feb8.174944.19838@netcom.com> mandel@netcom.com (Tom Mandel)
writes:

> I'm very well aware that not everyone
>operates with the same set of ethics.  For instance, if you ask the
>kid "hackers" who hang out in #hack on IRC (Internet Relay Channel),
>their answer will be something along the lines of "It's okay to take
>anything that's been made publicly available."

I think a more accurate phrasing of this "kid hacker" ethic is, "It's okay
to take anything that's physically available." When a site doesn't put locks
on files, but sends a notice that must be read when you enter a directory
which says "these files are private, and not for public use" then they
haven't been made publicly available. If I don't lock my front door, that
doesn't mean I've made the contents of my house publicly available. That
cyberspace makes some things easy, and perhaps even anonymous doesn't change
the legal and ethical situation, yes?

Further, intellectual property law makes it clear that just because an
original isn't taken (that is--that the owner isn't deprived of his
original) doesn't mitigate the crime of theft.

If one agrees with the above, than one may go further, to paraphrase the
"kid hacker ethic" to "It's okay to steal anything that's not tied down." In
other words, though wrapping themselves in rationalization and intellectual
defense, isn't the kid hacker ethic, at bottom, nothing more than a defense
of common theft?

To avoid flames, assume nobody corresponding here is a kid hacker, so this
message need not be take personally by anyone.

That brings one to the next interesting question, though it may more
properly belong in alt.child.raising. How can one persuade (that is, by what
arguments, if any) _rational_ "kid hackers" to desist from the above behavior,
if it is possible so to do by rational advocacy?

