In article <1993Feb8.181723.24822@netcom.com> strnlght@netcom.com (David Sternlight) writes:
>That brings one to the next interesting question, though it may more
>properly belong in alt.child.raising. How can one persuade (that is, by what
>arguments, if any) _rational_ "kid hackers" to desist from the above behavior,
>if it is possible so to do by rational advocacy?

This is an interesting problem.  I doubt very much that the approch
currently being taken--increasing legal sanctions--will do much, except
to force the marginal crackers out of the game.

I recently read--where?--that a telco or some organization like that
was getting involved in the computer/communications ethics picture
by setting up courses, or something like that, to teach young kids
just starting out with computers about the "right" set of ethics.

But I think the problem is really "a generation gap" problem.  That is,
young men are always looking around for some way to show off, to stand
out against "authority."  In the 1950s we had hot-rodding, later on
there was dope, and these days one of the ways of rebelling against
one's elders is to crack computer systems.

Another aspect of the problem is that of the declining credibility of
societal institutions in general.  Back in the 1950s and 1960s, most
of us respected large companies (like Ma Bell); these days, general
trust in institutions has declined quite a bit and that overall
attitude weakens rational arguments to "kid hackers."

My general thinking is that this is not solely an ethical problem;
it has to be tackled from lots of different angles at once:
better education to start with, good parenting, much more attention
to really effective security, appropriate (not excessive) sanctions,
and so on.  And sometimes I think it would be a good idea for a group
of companies to set up a nice open part of the network so that
rambunctious teenage boys--there are very few women crackers--could
play around to their heart's content.

