Tit for tat.  But I think, minor flames aside here, the nature
of the problem or confusion about what's right is being nicely
demonstrated.  

Interestingly, it is not too hard to get across the point that
viewing or using another person's computer files without permission
is bad behavior.  Of course, different kinds of users might
interpret very differently what "authorized permission" means.
A long-time UNIX user might well say that any file with read or 
execute permissions set to all users is fine to read or execute; a
neophyte to computers might feel shocked and invaded if he or she
finds out that someone read his -rw--r--r- files.

What is "authorized permission" to one is an unwelcome invasion of
privacy to another.

There are NO hard and fast rules here.  We have all been taught
different things for different situations.

So...how to resolve this?


