In article <C25ovs.Hw1@demon.co.uk> gtoal@pizzabox.demon.co.uk (Graham Toal) writes:
>In article <C25BJw.B9x@news.iastate.edu> john@iastate.edu (John Hascall) writes:
>:Hogwash.  You did nothing wrong.  Anonymous FTP is a service for making
>:files available to other people -- if the boneheads at ai.sri.com don't
>:understand this, that's hardly our (the rest of us) problem.
>
>UK may be different.  When I had a commercial account at Edinburgh
>University, I had to sign a piece of paper which effectively said
>'just because a file on this system has public read permission, doesn't
>mean you're allowed to look at it', along with all the usual veiled
>threats of prosecution if you did.  And that was a system which not only
>logged every command you typed, but had people going over the logs too.

	These are both examples of the general difficulty of defining
what, exactly, is an "authorized user," and how one can tell if one is
"authorized" for a particular form of access.  In the past, this perhaps
wasn't such a big deal, but now that (in the US) there are criminal
penalties associated with "accessing (???) a computer without authorization,"
it becomes more important to pin this down.

	At Edinburg, how was a user supposed to know what files he was
"authorized" to access?  Just those owned by the user?  (But what about
the man pages?)  If world-readable permissions on a file didn't constitute
"authorization," what did?

	At SRI, if the fact that the ftpd would accept connections from
"anonynmous" didn't constitute "authorized access," what does?  Appealing
to the "breaking-and-entering" analogy, while in most states I can't
enter private dwellings without an invitation, even if the door is unlocked,
this obviously doesn't apply to department stores.  SRI's anonymous ftp
site is like a huge sign saying "WELCOME TO SRI!!!" with "Enter" and
"Exit" signs on swinging double doors.  I don't wait in front of Sears
until some doorman invites me in, or call them to mention that I'm
planning to pick up some paint; I just go.

	If I finger "guest" at some machine, and it tells me:

        Login name: guest          In real life: Guest Account

is this "authorization" to login as "guest" in order to be a guest user
on that machine?  If I do, and there is no password, am I an authorized
user?

	In general, for which services are the general Internet community
"authorized" for on my machine?  Should I consider myself "authorized"
to send mail to any machine on the Internet?  (i.e., connect to the
SMTP port?)  Why should I consider myself "authorized" to send mail and
not DEBUG messages?  What about "finger?"  What about "nfsd?"

	Hell, if I can't consider the fact that SRI's ftpd will allow
me access as "authorization," how can I use anonymous ftp anywhere?

