====== ftp'ed with permission from 
             ai.sri.comp/pub/comp.org.eff.talk-response-incident ====
=========================================================================
							6-Feb-93
	Response to messages on the alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk
	  and comp.org.eff.talk newsgroups
		Part 1 - ~ftp/etc/passwd incident

Hi...  I manage the computer facilities at AI.SRI.COM.  Submittors to this
newsgroup have expressed opinion of their interpretation of our anonymous
FTP setup.  Before expressing their opinions, none of these people bothered
to contact us about our rationale.  Maybe I can add some facts to the
opinions expressed.  

Because of the length, this response is broken into two pieces.  This one
deals with the incident of someone retrieving "/etc/passwd".  The second
deals with our policy regarding anonymous FTP access.


Today, I did finally receive one message (reasonably addressed to
POSTMASTER) about this but I didn't realize there was an on-going
discussion until later this morning when third-hand we heard about the
discussion.

-------
  An aside about addressing mail to responsible individuals:

If you contact NIC.DDN.MIL, and issue a "whois AI.SRI.COM", you will see
that I am listed as the coordinator.  That is the correct address to use
for administrative issues.  The address "POSTMASTER" is properly used for
issues dealing with mail delivery (per RFC822).  If you contact the domain
name server for the domain AI.SRI.COM, you will also find my address in the
SOA record.  That is the correct address for DNS problems or issues.  Any
of these addresses are acceptable if you can't find the one you need to
use.  "root" is hopelessly Un*x-specific and, in my opinion, should be
avoided at all costs.

-------

While we don't wish to belabor the incident that sparked the series of
messages, we feel we should respond to charges of over-reaction by our
site.  So we would like to clarify what happened in so far as we know it.
We do not know what went on internal to the foreign site.


On Thursday morning (4-Feb-93), someone accessed a password file which,
while containing no sensitive information, could (on an improperly
configured site) contain sensitive information.  In our view, this is like
someone grabbing your empty wallet off a table.  They didn't get anything
but they *may* be going around grabbing other wallets.  (Please understand
I am specifically NOT accusing anyone of stealing sensitive information
from anywhere.)

That afternoon we sent to the authorities at the originating site the
following notification:

>TO:	<name removed>
>
>You are listed in the NIC database as the technical contact for your
>site.  I am sending this note to you because we observed suspicious 
>activity that seems to involve your site.  Please acknowledge receipt 
>of this message and verify that we have indeed contacted the resposible 
>system adminstrator. (You may want to update this information with
>the NIC if you are no longer the technical contact at this site, You
>might also want to update you bitnet address.)
>
>We logged an annonymous ftp connection from you site this morning
>at 6:18PST.  During this session there was an attempt to access
>our site's passwd file.  This is activity that is very often associated
>with hackers.  The user supplied the following information as
>a password when the annonymous ftp connection was established:
>
>     <"password" removed, of the form "<user>@<domain>">
>
>Please investigate this incident and respond.
>
>  <signed by an AIC computer facility staff member>

Please note that we do not say that this person did anything improper at
our site.  (Actually, I consider the person's actions improper but not
something that in and of itself warrants administrative action.)  Also,
note that we do not say the user name given in the password had anything to
do with the incident.  Indeed, a malicious cracker would most like give
misleading information.  A naive, non-malicious user would most likely give
his own name.  In an innocuous case such as this may be, the system admin
should probably contact the user, find out whether he was the one that
did this, and, if so, inform him that snooping passwd files off foreign
systems is inappropriate conduct that is hard to distinguish from
possibly illegal conduct.

By the way, that person retrieved only two files that morning: /pub/README and
/etc/passwd.  He did not retrieve any other files (AI-related or otherwise).

It is our policy that when we find anyone doing something to our system
that we consider inappropriate, we will contact a responsible person at
that site.  In fact, if we truly suspect a cracker is attempting to break
into our system, we do not send mail to the system administrator, we use
the telephone because we do not know that the cracker isn't monitoring and
editing the system administrator's mail.

Why not contact the purported individual?  Because he may be a cracker and
we have let him onto the fact that he has been spotted (so he may run
elsewhere).  Or he may be a novice and not understand what is going on (and
his account is being used by someone else).  Or he may be hard to contact.
Or, in the case of anonymous FTP, the name given may not be a valid
account.  In fact, we have seen cases where such reporting to the
appropriate individual led to a site discovering that a cracker had broken
into their site and was using it to break into others.

Since the posting of "I've verified that on least one computer at SRI, a
file called "passwd" is publically available via anonymous ftp.", we've had
a number of attempts at getting the passwd file.  Since we have no way of
knowing which of those attempts are from honest, upstanding citizens of the
net who are simply interested in academic freedom, etc., and which might be
from crackers who were looking for accounts and passwords to break into, we
will follow up on these actions by a message much like the above.

In order to avoid escalation of this incident at your site any higher than
the first level, I suggest that any of you who are in the first category,
contact your system administrators to let them know such a note is coming
and that it was you that retrieved the password (or /etc/group file).
You can explain the circumstances under which you did it.  Perhaps he will
understand and drop the matter there.

If you are in the first category and tried to do something fancy (you
know who you are), you'd better be ready to explain that to your system
administrator.

If you are in the second category, well, I suspect you'll do just the same
but, of course, no crackers are reading this newsgroup!


