========= ai.sri.comp:pub/comp.org.eff.talk-response-policy =========

							8-Feb-93
	Response to messages on the alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk
	  and comp.org.eff.talk newsgroups
		Part 2 - AI.SRI.COM's Anonymous FTP access policy


Hi...  I manage the computer facilities at AI.SRI.COM.  Submittors to this
newsgroup have expressed opinion of their interpretation of our anonymous
FTP setup.  Before expressing their opinions, none of these people bothered
to contact us about our rationale.  Maybe I can add some facts to the
opinions expressed.  

Because of the length, this response is broken into two pieces.  This, the
second one, deals with our policy regarding anonymous FTP access.  The
first one deals with the incident of someone retrieving "/etc/passwd".

Since our site does not store the alt.* newsgroups, I'm afraid I don't have
the original posting nor all of the postings.  I've only seen the postings
on comp.org.eff.talk.  I could probably pick up those other postings
tomorrow but I wanted to get this out today.

On this newsgroup were several messages that questioned our policy of
stating that the files in ~ftp/pub were not public.  We wish to explain the
policy and the reasons behind it.  Comments are welcomed and should be
addressed to "Tyson@AI.SRI.COM".  I will read each one.  I doubt I'll be
able to respond to each of them individually.  Flames will be treated
appropriately.

As you well know, anonymous FTP is used to allow people at one site to
transfer files to or from another site without actually having an account
at the second site.  In particular, this is an easy way to distribute
data to a potentially unlimited group of individuals.  You do not have to
give each of them an account (and securely tell them the password).

Anonymous FTP is also used in a second way, as a way of publishing files
for the general public.  Sharing information is truly one of the most
beneficial practices on the Internet.  

We are a research group whose members are engaged in a variety of
collaborative efforts with a wide range of research groups at universities
and companies.  We use anonymous FTP from time to time to make information
available to those collaborators.  In that sense, we are using anonymous
FTP in the first way.  Since nothing in our anonymous FTP account is of
general interest to the net, we request that uninvited individuals and
archive retrieval programs not connect to us.  In other words, the
data we make available for anonymous FTP is not being published to the
general public.

Our policy is not arbitrary, but was instituted in response to specific
problems.  At various times, we have had many megabytes of data files that
were of interest only to the various collaborators on a project.  One
night, an automatic retrieval program from overseas made close to 200
attempts to retrieve a 2 MB file that was not intended to be public.  We
would prefer to avoid being a participant, no matter how unwilling, in such
an abuse of network resources.

At other times, we have found our files listed as being publicly available
when that was not our intent.  Someone's automatic program came by, and
because they could read our anonymous FTP directory, presumed the files
were public.  They weren't and aren't.  My front yard isn't public territory
just because I haven't fenced it in.


Our policy of requesting that only invited people access our anonymous
FTP directory also makes it easier for us to track our distributions.
One typical situation is that a researcher will announce the availability
of a file to a small group of collaborators or colleagues.  He or she
will then give the system administrators a list of who is expected to
pick up the file, and ask to be notified about who did and didn't get
it.  The researcher can then follow up with the groups that don't get
it.  Providing this service to our users is much harder if our logs are
cluttered by the activities of dozens of random browsers each night.

Because we cannot stop automatic retrieval programs from connecting to
us, we have had to institute a policy of hiding the names of most files
in our anonymous FTP directory.  We did not want to do this because we
knew that it would cause problems for legitimate users of our anonymous
FTP, but it was the only practical solution to the abusive browser.
An analogy is the common public library policy of limiting the number
of books an individual can check out and limiting the time they can be
kept.  This policy protects the rights of legitimate users from the
abusive user who would check out and keep enormous numbers of books on
the off chance he might want to look at them. Similarly, we found that
we needed to protect the rights of legitimate users to have adequate CPU
cycles and network bandwidth for their activities.


Regarding the naming of directories "~ftp/*".  The documentation provided
by Sun only mentions this in the FTPD(8C) where it lists /bin, /etc,
/usr, /dev, and /pub.  For the last it says "Users should then place files
which are to be accessible via the anonymous account in this directory."
Nowhere does is it suggested that by doing so, these files are published
to the entire world.

However, it does seem that there is a large segment of the population who
believe that "pub" means public (rather than an English drinking
establishment :-).  I suspect that we may be forced to rename our ~ftp/pub
to be something like ~ftp/pri.  The files in it will still be available for
anonymous FTP.  Someone intent on stealing data won't be bothered by the
name change.


The internet of today is not the network that I first used in 1973.  There
are too many (ab)users to be able to trust everyone.  I have resisted
efforts to have our site go the way of many in allow only mail and
name service access as the only incoming network connections.  
At least so far.


