In the "Legal Tangles During FTP at SRI" thread, several people pointed out
that anonymous FTP is considered to be an open invitation for net people to
wander around your files.  They couldn't understand why an organization
would be so inconsistent as to set up an anonymous FTP area and then say
that only authorized access is permitted.  I thought I'd point out a
perfectly reasonable justification.

Often one needs to make a file available to a colleague on the net.  If
it's not too long you would just email it.  But if it's big, FTP is a much
nicer mechanism.  However, the colleague may not have an account on your
machine, and authorizing one for one or two file transfers is usually more
work than it's worth.  So you put it in your anonymous FTP area and send
your colleague a pointer to it.  This technology doesn't permit you to
restrict access so only that colleague can access it, yet it is still the
case that he is the only one authorized to take it.

This is not a contrived example.  On several occasions I've made crash
dumps available to a vendor support organization this way.

When archie was first implemented, it automatically tried to access and
catalogue every FTP server it could find.  Many sites objected, since they
didn't really consider themselves "public access".  The archie maintainers
changed things so that it only catalogued sites that gave permission.
