It is interesting to note the ironies here.  SRI is a .COM domain
and it is indeed a private company, a leading research and consulting
firm, and its AI group is one of the oldest in the world.  SRI is
also one of the firms responsible for creating the Internet itself,
having played a pivotal role in the development and management of
the ARPANET.  (That is another part of SRI, not AI.SRI.COM.)

I keep feeling inclined to take both sides in this debate.  SRI
is within its rights to protect the privacy of its files and its
computers...and the way AI.SRI.COM is handling this doesn't seem
quite optimal.

I gather, from another posting in this thread, that there is software
available for limiting general access to anonymous ftp, and my hunch
is that as the network grows, that sort of solution, rather than honor,
ethics, and other social approaches will be how this sort of issue
is solved.

