>>>>> "Carl" == Carl M. Kadie <kadie@cs.uiuc.edu> writes:

	Carl> This discussion seems to be mostly about differing
	Carl> expectations.  I wonder if there are some things we can 
	Carl> agree on.

	Carl> Anonymous ftp users:
	Carl> * 1) obey any restrictions of which you receive notice --
	Carl>      you are a guest. If you believe the restrictions to be
	Carl>      unreasonable criticize them if you wish, but not violate
	Carl>      them. 

Hmm.  This suggests that a sysadmin who wishes to restrict access to
~ftp:/etc/passwd, or who views it as more sensitive than any other ftp-able
file, should post an explicit warning notice to that effect.  I think that
would be a good idea.  (The ai.sri.com folks seem to regard ~ftp:/etc/group
as sensitive too; I'll refrain from comment.)

Warning notices on subdirectories of ~ftp should really make clear what it
is they're threatening: social opprobium (e.g., an indignant Usenet posting
reporting ``A bozo from foobar.edu ftp'd our ~ftp:/etc/group!'') or
stronger action.  Giving reasons would be a good idea too; ``We don't want
you doing this'' is unsatisfactory.

I realize those recommendations are rather strong.  But in general the
burden should be on the sysadmin to clarify, and insofar as possible
justify, his site's position.  That's because any restrictions whatever are
historically extremely unusual, and liable to confuse people.

	Carl>   2) do not go out of your way to avoid receiving notice of
	Carl>      restrictions 

Increasingly people accessing remote files via ftp use a front-end program,
rather than the conventional ftp client.  I myself use the ange-ftp
package, which runs under GNU Emacs.  I can obtain listings of remote
directories, and view remote files, just as easily as I can list local
directories and view local files.  The interface is exactly the same.

The punchline: ange-ftp users won't see the warning messages on login.  The
current release of the ange-ftp package doesn't support that.  The user
doesn't `log in' at all; it's done automatically, without his typing a
username or password.

Comments, anyone?

	Carl>   4) On most system, public files are in the "pub" directory.
	Carl>      Avoid the other directories.

The ``public files = ~ftp:/pub'' convention is unfortunately _very_ far
from being adhered to.  There are lots of sites out there which don't place
files in /pub, or have both a /pub directory and miscellaneous other
directories, all for public consumption.

	Carl> Anonymous ftp administrators:
	Carl>   6) If a user violates your restrictions even after receiving 
	Carl>      notice, contact the user's sys admin and say: "Please
	Carl>      ask your user to observe the restrictions we place on our
	Carl>      anonymous ftp site." 

But how to guarantee that the notice is read?  I've already mentioned the
problems with ange-ftp.

The real problem is that sysadmins (including the folks at ai.sri.com) are
using a package, and even more a protocol, which was designed in an age that
was more open and trusting, and less technically advanced, than the present
one.  If the ftp protocol were designed from scratch today, it would surely
have more security hooks in it.  (Not to mention useful features like the
ability to retrieve a specified part of a file.)

	Carl>   7) When contacting the user's site, avoid mentioning the
	Carl>      ~ftp:/etc/passwd file; any such references are likely to be
	Carl>      misunderstood.

A very good idea.  It would probably be all right to mention
~ftp:/etc/group, though, if that was the file illicitly transferred.
(Note for the humor-impaired: yes, that was a joke.)

I see no easy solutions here.  As I said, ftp is a relic of another era; so
is anonymous ftp.  Let's keep that era alive as long as we can.

