In article <1993Feb5.031933.11448@eff.org> kadie@eff.org (Carl M. Kadie) writes:
>>}I'm enclosing the U. of Illinois office and dorm privacy policy. It
>>}distinguishes kinds of entry. Here is my summary:
>>}I. Search
>>}     Requires: a search warrant
>>}     For:      whatever you can get a search warrant for
>>}II. "Inspections"
>>}     Requires: 24-hour notice
>>}     For:     "to determine compliance with
>>}               provisions of applicable multiple-dwelling unit laws,
>>}               ordinances, and regulations, or for improvement or repairs"

>john@iastate.edu (John Hascall) writes:

>>So, the $64 question is, which is it: a "software search" or a "software
>>inspection"?

>I think a search is when 1) there is no notice of the time of the
>search/inspection or 2) no notice of exact nature of the
>search/inspection or 3) there is a change that personal material will
>be seen by people.

Carl, this seems to be based more on your pet prejudices about what
should and should not be allowable rather than on the meaning of the
English words 'search' and 'inspection'.  In particular, I think it is
quite obvious that it is possible to have a no notice inspection, an
inspection whose nature is not explained to the person inspected, and
even an inspection which may reveal personal material.

Personally, I think the defining line is how you look and what you
look for.  I might search a user's disk for a letter to the newspaper
saying that the vice chancellor is a pederast.  I would inspect the
disk for illicit copies of copyrighted software.  If I use grep or
some analog that's a search.  If I use ls that's an inspection.


From caf-talk Caf Feb 10 14:04:50 1993
Newsgroups: alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk,comp.org.eff.talk,misc.int-property
From: kadie@eff.org (Carl M. Kadie)
Subject: Re: SPA vs. Universities
Message-ID: <1993Feb10.190417.2962@eff.org>
Date: Wed, 10 Feb 1993 19:04:17 GMT

mfriedma@us.oracle.com (Michael Friedman) writes:

[...]
>Carl, this seems to be based more on your pet prejudices about what
>should and should not be allowable rather than on the meaning of the
>English words 'search' and 'inspection'.
[...]

I wasn't trying to distinguish between the ordinary use the word
"search" and ordinary use of the word "inspection". As you suggest
there is no such distinction because the meanings of the words
overlap.

[For convenience, let's define a new word, "searspection" that mean a
search or inspection or anything in between. In my previous notes, I
used the phrase "search/inspection".]

My goal was to characterize two types of searspections. My model was
the U. of Illinois privacy policy. A type 1 searspection requries
individualized suspicion and high-level authoriziation such as a
warrant. I believe the legal term for this "search". A type 2
searspection requires no individualized suspecion and usually no
individualized authorization. I didn't know what to call this so I
called it "'inspection'". I used scare quotes to suggest that this was
not the ordinary use of the word.

I am open to suggestions for better terminology. It was not my
intention to beg the question.

- Carl

