I've been following the discussion on the anon ftp 'incident' at
SRI.  Whether or not the student accessing the /etc/passwd file was
smart, ethical, innocent, etc. has generated some interesting
discussion.  The 'incident' raises some other questions for me that
I think are important.

The SRI site administrator has posted their official stand on this,
indicating the site is NOT for public anon ftp.  Tyson states,
"...the data we make available for anonymous FTP is not being
published to the general public."  SRI has a right to the privacy
of their files.  Do they also have the responsibility to notify an
anon ftp user that the machine they just logged onto is private? 
A previous poster has indicated that when last checked, there was
no opening message to announce the site as not for public traffic.

They went on to say the warning was in a README file, in a
directory called 'pub'.  After reading this, of course, the only
proper thing to do is logout immediately.  But this still results
in trespassing before you can prevent it.

Another previous poster told us that archie only accumulates data
from sites that give permission -  a change implemented apparently
for situations just like SRI's.  An archie query of pk* (e.g.,
pkzip) yields a response from that site.

I use ftp often, and now I wonder if I've inadvertently trespassed
on a system along the line without even knowing it.  I use archie
to locate files, and anon ftp to get them.  I never precede the
anon password with '-', thus the opening message is displayed, and
pay attention to the README files at the top level.  Sounds like
this would not have prevented me from accidentally trespassing on
SRI's machine.  

As a student, I am perhaps overly sensitive to this kind of stuff. 
We all know how accusations and suspicions can grow into monstrous
situations - in many situations one is considered guilty until
proven innocent.  Last year, at a different school, I received a
nastygram concerning a file called 'passwords' in my account.  It
was a collection of text references and treatises on how NOT to
choose a password.  I guess the sysadmin didn't check on what it
was, and wrote "get rid of the password file, or I will control
you".  At the bottom of the message was the ominous cc:...a copy
had been sent to my instructor, and the computer center
administrator.  Made me want to reach out and touch someone.  

