Three comments:
  1) making an anonymous FTP directory is IMHO sorta like putting
newsletters in a magazine rack in the publicly accessible lobby of
a company building. You can say "please do not take this information"
but you shouldn't be surprised if people do. Of course, a person
reading the sign should cooperate. 

  2) There is something special about the passwd file. 
Even though taking the passwd file is not itself a crime, it really is
snooping and sites which responded by asking the originating site to 
perhaps gently warn their user are not over-reacting. If someone here
is using our computers to collect passwd files from other systems, we
might ask them to stop. If we caught someone running crack on passwd
files from other systems, they would be in trouble (this IS explicitly
spelled out, not in my system policy, but in the University's Student
Code of Conduct) 

  3) It seems to me that making a special ftp account, perhaps with a
limited shell, and making the passwd available to legitimate users, is
a good way around these problems. Even if the password becomes well
known, logging onto the system with a username of, say SRIclient,
with a passwd other than "guest" and with a login message saying "this
account is for authorized customers and employees only" would clearly
establish intent.


