In article <1le1ee$neg@nz12.rz.uni-karlsruhe.de> s_titz@ira.uka.de (Olaf
Titz) writes:

>
>Okay, this becomes a rather philosophical question, but: What makes
>the crime of theft, given the above statement? Usually, we think that
>theft is punished because the damage that is done by theft is the
>deprivation of the owner of his legal possession... or what?
>

Exactly. Under intellectual property law, the "legal posession" of an owner
of a patent (for example) includes the right to charge others for using it.
Thus even if a user doesn't deprive the original patent holder of his
original patented physical object, he is being deprived of the right to
charge that user--thus deprived of his legal possession.

David
