David Sternlight argues that depriving one of the ability to sell a product
to another constitutes theft. Perhaps some of the copyright attorneys can 
correct me, but it's my understanding that the "right" to sell isn't a
property right, and deprivation of it does not, of itself, constitute
theft (a felony), but, at worst, a civil offense.

It should also be noted that possession of an unpurchased sofware program
(as an example) hardly deprives the author of a sale. If I test a
program and like it, I will buy it. Where does deprivation or theft enter in
here?

Jim Thomas



