>>In article <1993Feb8.181723.24822@netcom.com> strnlght@netcom.com (David
Sternlight) writes:

>>
>>> Further, intellectual property law makes it clear that just because an
>>> original isn't taken (that is--that the owner isn't deprived of his
>>> original) doesn't mitigate the crime of theft.
>
>David Sternlight argues that depriving one of the ability to sell a product
>to another constitutes theft.

This is a distortion of what I said. There is a legal and property right
embodied in, for example, patents. Thus one is not depriving another of the
"ability" to sell a product, but of a property right. That's quite a
different matter. Note that a copyright is also a property right. One may
buy, sell, and license such copyrights. The right being deprived here is an
ownership/property right of the copyright/patent holder. Your introduction
of the "right to sell" into this discussion is a large red herring and a
piece of legal nonsense.

As to whether copyright violations are covered by civil vs. criminal law,
many things we think of as crimes are, in reality, covered by civil law.
Trespassing is an example. That's a clear violation of a property right, yet
the remedy is to sue, not call the police. I don't intend to get into legal
nit-picking in this general discussion, as to whether taking someone's
property is "theft" the codified felony, or "theft" the ethical crime. When
my mother taught me about stealing she did not have a copy of Blackstone
next to her. Your message appears to be one of the many in a series of
rationalizations for software piracy we've seen time and time again. It
hasn't convinced anyone in the past and won't now.

>It should also be noted that possession of an unpurchased sofware program
>(as an example) hardly deprives the author of a sale. If I test a
>program and like it, I will buy it. Where does deprivation or theft enter in
>here?
>

This is another piece of childish rationalization offered for years for
piracy. It won't wash. Nobody cares if you "might" buy a pirated program you
like. The only people with status to permit such behavior are the owners of
the copyrights themselves, and they have repeatedly rejected this argument
out of hand.

David
>


