tk0jut1@mvs.cso.niu wrote:
 
>>It is clear from your comments that I have not distorted your position.
>>You conflate two kinds of "rights" and continue with the non sequitor that
>>one doesn't except *your* view, it necessarily promotes a straw argument
>>you posit and attribute to others. Your comment is also inaccurate:
 
David Sternlight wrote:
 
>What, specifically are you saying? Let's see some evidence. The above
>is empty posturing without specifics.
 
You conflated property rights and intellectual property rights.
You were corrected, and you acknowledged the correction. The
conflation is the basis of your argument, and when it fails, your
argument fails.
 
>>1) The only people with status to permit such behavior are not the copyright
>>   owners, but legislators and law enforcement officials.
 
>Untrue. Legislators have already acted to pass patent and
>copyright laws which are contrary to the "pirate's argument." Law
>enforcement officials enforce the existing law, not the law as
>criminals and civil violators wish it to be. The law has given
>the right to decide to the copyright owner, not the pirate.
>Unless and until the law is changed that's all she wrote.
 
Can you cite a law that gives "the right to decide" to the copyright
owner? Your wording suggests that you believe that a copyright owner
has the right to assert claims, whether legislatively enacted or not,
and the law then honors the assertions. Statutes are more ambiguous
and transient than you seem to recognize.
 
You speak of the "pirate's argument." What is this argument, and to
whom do you attribute it? Your logic and rhetoric suggest that
one who opposed the death penalty would advocate murder by
promoting a "murder's argument."
 
>>2) The latitude for "fair use" for software is far more restrictive than
>   for other creative works.
 
>So what?
 
You argue on the abstract, but unclearly defined, issue of "theft." On
one hand you call legal distinctions between criminal and civil law
"nitpicking," and on the other you appeal to law when convenient. In
your appeals to law, it's useful to remember that the permissible
scope of copying and use for some media is less restrictive than for
others. This inconsistency confuses both the legal and abstract
definitions of "theft." It also suggests that meanings are not as
clear-cut as you claim.
 
>>3) Despite your claim that you find distinctions between civil and
>>   criminal law as "nitpicking," you promote criminalization of
>>  "unauthorized" copying and dismiss as a "childish rationalization"
>>  views that suggest alternative perspectives.
 
>It is not an "alternate perspective" to argue that taking the
>property of others without permission is o.k. because if you like
>it you'll buy it. It is a rationalization of the crook, pure and
>simple.
 
I said "alternative," a small, but not inconsequential difference if
you wish to pick at a nuance. The fact that you recognize a view in
opposition to your certainly suggests an *alternative* position (as I
understand the meaning of the word).
 
Dr Johnsons modest proposal for the poor was certainly an
*alternative*, and one not entirely without approximate historical
precedent.
 
Please cite for me any place, at any time, when I have ever said that
"taking the property of others without permission is ok." I do not
agree that possession of software that I did not purchase is
*necessarily* taking the property of others.  There is considerable
room for intellectual disagreement on this, and labelling others
"pirates" and "thieves" and imputing illegal or unethical motives to
others may be cathartic, but it's questionable logic.
 
I've worked among "crooks" for much of life, and I've never, not once,
heard one use the argument that "I stole it because if I liked it, I'd
buy it." Can you cite a source for your claim that's it's a
rationalization of crooks "pure and simple?"
 
>Using your logic, Dr. Johnson had an "alternate perspective" on
>remedying the condition of the poor. There is something called
>right and wrong, and the arguments of the pirate are nothing
>short of those of the ethical defective.
 
Your predilection for ad hominem is showing. My arguments are those
of a concerned user who believes in the rights of both sides.
 
>>Both users and authors have rights. The question is: How does one establish
>>a just balance?
 
>Users have no rights unless they have purchased a product and are
>using it legally and consistent with any laws or agreements
>involved.  Pirates have no rights. Your sentence obfuscates by
>lumping legal users with pirates.
 
At stake is how to enact laws that recognize the rights of users.  In
your view, once a law or "agreement" (even if invalid) is in place, it
is unethical and illogical to raise a challenge.  I, and at least some
attorneys (See CuD #5.06) suggest that, for example, shrinkwrap
licenses are not binding. In your logic, if I make install a copy of a
program, make a backup to disk, and another precautionary backup to
tape, I am unethical, a pirate, and a thief, because most shrinkwrap
licenses limit backups to a *single* copy.
 
>This discussion is pointless. It is a repeat of the piracy
>discussion that pops up every so often and almost everywhere. The
>pirates and their apologists don't accept logic or ethics, and
>those of us who believe in obeying the law or trying to get it
>changed, but not taking it into our own hands, are shouting into
>an ethical vacuum to no effect.
 
Your logic, as near as I can follow it, is this:  You assume that any
deviation from *your* idiosyncratic standards is a major
transgression, you label deviants as "pirates" and "thieves" and then
argue to the label rather than the issues, and you then complain about
pointless discussions. Is there an irony here, or am I missing
something?  How does one enact or alter laws without discussion?
 
I have said repeatedly: If one uses a commercial or shareware software
program, then one is obligated to pay for it.  But, this hardly means
that opposition to zero-tolerance on something called "piracy" (like
"hacking," an absurdly misused term) is unethical. The SPA defines
piracy as any deviation from the "one installation/one backup"
shrinkwrap limitations.  If anecdotal accounts are any indication,
this definition makes "pirates" out of many, probably most, PC users,
and it would also seem to violate the users' rights of ownership of
property that they have (or will) purchase.
 
Discussion of the legitimate underlying issues is hardly pointless.
 
Jim Thomas
 



