I think Carl Kadie's analogy of an open, public shop was the best
among many analogies suggested here.

Yes, you are expected to behave reasonably in a shop, but you wouldn't
expect, as Kadie suggested, to be arrested for trespassing for merely
entering or (my addition) to be held accountable for looking at items
that appear to be on display for sale.

And (again my addition) you wouldn't expect to be accused of
shoplifting for putting something on a counter marked "Free -- Take
One" in your pocket, even if it turned out there was some
misunderstanding; your assumption was reasonable and it was just that,
a misunderstanding at worst, and quite possibly the proprietor's fault
for being confusing (e.g.  putting a sticker on some items that said
"this one isn't free" that you hadn't noticed.)

And without going into stretches like wandering into employees-only
areas which I don't think is relevant it's a good analogy.

An anonymous FTP site is by and large a big "Free - Take One" sign. If
that doesn't fit the bill then I think you (the sysadmin) have to find
another approach, not get frustrated at people who take you at your
word or assume common conventions are in effect.

Let's keep in mind that this started over the SRI admin who believed
that it was unreasonable that people thought a directory whose name
was pub/ meant "public" (i.e. free, take one), which is certainly what
pub/ means on thousands of other anon ftp sites on the net.

