ftp, and the curious will be left with very clear solid boundaries
of where they may roam.

In the meantime, roamers have this incredible freedom and license.
Whether you hold to a constrained view of this freedom is, alas,
irrelevant.  Roamers will roam and hackers will hack.  But don't
call me an outlaw just because I seem to behave like a hacker and
not like a roamer.  Believe me, I have hacked, and this ain't it.

What our friend did was questionable, but it is hard to condemn him,
given the loosey-goosey nature of the net, especially anonymous ftp, 
that opened up this moral grey zone.  Perhaps on philosophical
grounds alone you condemn him anyway.  As a *practical* matter,
however, it is really hard for me to hear folx applauding his getting
in trouble with the three most important academic people in his life.

Elizabeth Schwartz, like most women, has a good idea of what kind of
power and muscle she can bring to bear on troublesome users if she
needs to.  Instead, she complicates things by trying to negotiate with
these users and bring them into some consensus with the vision of the
internet, or at least with the minimal expectations U Mass-Boston has
for its net (tho' I would say that contributes to the larger vision).  

Pardon me everybody for harping so much on this, but I wish yall
would rush to understand and resolve the situation.  Rushing to use
force resolves nothing.  There are many places in this society where
the hard cops could probably hand their badges over for good to the
soft cops and things would improve measurably.

Aaron

