No.  The exact situation (from what I understand) was more equivalent to
having the message written in one of those brochures on the table:  A file
called README in /pub.  No message on login...the message wasn't even in
the top level directory.  I wholeheartedly agree that once one had
read that file one should abide by SRI's wishes (it IS their machine).
The whole question revolves around SRI not making their intent *clear*:

1) allowing ANONYMOUS ftp (as opposed to "SRIclient" + e-mail address for
   a password)

2) no automagic presentation of README upon login...this can't be hard
   since the majority of the ftp sites I visit do it.  Admittedly this
   gets bypassed if you login using the "-", but at least they could
   argue that they had made clear their intent and then it would be
   the user's fault to being too blind to read the notice.

3) keeping the file structure the same where "everyone" (but them) 
   appears to believe that /pub == public.  Again, it's a contention
   of "intent".  Something as simple as renaming it /client_code would
   give them a LOT more ground to stand on.

4) having the README only in the sub-directory (many anonymous ftp
   sites don't build the whole file structure under /pub so it's
   easy to imagine /pub being the _last_ place someone looks at).

So, I think my analogy is still quite good.  And that the majority
(not necessarily _all_) the grief they've incurred was of their own
doing and could easily have been prevented.

