You've lost me. The shrinkwrap license denotes what you claim is a binding
contract between user/author. I am giving you a concrete definition:
One who violates the license is a "pirate," and I'm using the SPA's
position on this. Are you saying that their definition is too narrow?
When you repeatedly assert that it's wrong to *take* intellectual property
without permission, it's hardly unreasonable to ask for more than the
vague generalities you've provided, and I've helped you providing one
specific definition. 

When I test out a software program and purchase it, that's hardly "taking
without permission." Whether it's an infringment is a separate issue.
Note that the SPA strongly opposes software rental (why is renting software
any less ethical than, say, renting a video?). For David Sternlight, this
constitutes piracy, and the malefactor then acquires the set of attributes
associated with evil-doers. If one continued to use a program without 
purchasing it, I would agree with parts of his argument. However, his view
of "taking without permission" is sufficiently restrictive that it leaves no
room for *alternative* views. He also seems to reject my right, as a consumer,
to make more than a single backup. He fails to explain why this is "taking
property." 

>I've shown by quoting Webster that the standards are neither mine nor
>idiosyncratic, but commonly accepted social standards of such weight that
>their usage is codified on a widely-used and respected dictionary. Your
>attempt at an ad hominem therefore fails, as do most of your other
