*sigh* this is what you get when people confuse computers with real
life.

in real life, freedom is the default. unless you make a lot of effort 
to lock up your belongings they are freely available to everyone. to 
keep people from abusing that property of reality our society has a 
common convention of property that proclaims that things default to 
the "owner". (some societies don't follow this convention.)

computers have a very definite set of generally highly enforced rules 
regarding property. it is very, very easy for someone to restrict
access to a piece of information. if i don't want you to read my mail,
i simply set it to not readable by you. in fact, to allow you to
read something of mine i must -explicitly- give you permission to do
so. as such, the cultural conventions of the net dictate that if 
something is readable, it was -meant- to be read, because otherwise 
the owner would have not lifted the standard restrictions.

SRI is breaking those conventions. that's fine, but they shouldn't 
complain when they encounter a bit of culture shock.

i'll agree with Mr. Barnhart. if something is set readable or made
available to the general public it should be assumed that the public
will read it. if you don't want that to happen, then don't make it
available to anyone who isn't supposed to have it.

