To get borrower information, you would not necessarily have to get a
librarian to cooperate.  Some libraries operate as follows:

	1. You take the book to the check-out, and the person there
	removes a card from a pocket in the book.  The card contains
	the filing information for the book.

	2. Your library card and the card from the book are placed in a
	machine that stamps your library card number and the due date on
	the card from the book.

	3. They file the card, and give you the book.

	4. When you return the book, they take the card from their
	"books checked out" file and put it back in the book and
	reshelve it.

Note that the book on the shelf contains the card with the (library card
number, due date) pairs.  Thus, someone who wants to obtain the library
card numbers of people who have recently checked out a book can just
walk into the library and look.

So if they can find some way to find out your library card number, they've
got you!

(It's interesting to look at these cards, by the way.  I visited the library
in the small town where I went to high school, and looked at some of my
favorite books from those days.  In many cases, my number was still in there,
even though it had been over 15 years -- not enough people had checked out
these books to fill up the card.  If library card information had been public,
I might have tracked down some of those people.  It would be interesting to
find out who the one other person was who had checked out the advanced
calculus book, and ask him/her what they were doing stuck in that small
town!

It's also interesting to note that if you wanted to figure out what books
I read at that library, you could, even without getting the library to
disclose my library card number.  All you would have to do was find out
that in high school I was interested in mathematics, astronomy, and
telescope making.  If you look at the books they have on those fields, you
will find only one library card number on the more advanced books in
each of those subjects.  That's me.)

--Tim Smith

