In this and subsequent paragraphs, which I have mercifully deleted, Mr.
Swaim attempts to lead us on a wild goose chase for hypothetical situations
that waste our energy and, intentionally or not, steer us completely away
from the matter at hand -- which is, to refresh your memory, whether SRI
has acted as a responsible citizen of the net commmunity.

When viewed through the lens of what really happened, rather than what
Mr. Swaim dreams it to be, we can meet these silly digressions before
they gain steam and become train wrecks.  So okay, Mr. Langston could
have been a dupe and someone had lied about his i.d.  But in fact he
admits freely to doing all that they said he did -- but not maliciously
or *even* illicitly.  Some have disagreed on that latter point but no
one until now has suggested SRI should have been so roundabout and
sneaky as they were.

Only a paranoid introverted fool would utterly disregard the option of
SRI contacting Mr. Langston himself -- tho' certainly SRI could've used
a Memphis State sysadmin as their intermediary.  Instead they sent a 
message and felt they'd served the ball into MSU's court, and didn't
bother to pursue it further.  This seriously undermines the open-systems,
free-exchange, verify-but-trust spirit that I experience every day on
the Internet.  It's a mockery of that remarkable creation, the anonymous
ftp site, and a victory for the forces of paranoia, which are of course
considerable, as can be seen from the success of alt.fan.rush-limbaugh.

Aaron

