Well, Tom, I'm sorry "we" feel that way, but unfortunately "we" have
a moral and intellectual responsibility to speak out on matters even
when "we" don't feel like it and decide "we" are going to replace
serious discussion with pedantic fault-finding over technical terms.

I think I know what you want me to say.  Something like, "Yes, I am
:pretty much aware of what sort of problems sysadmins there have to
:deal with in the way of inappropriate uses by outsiders.  Nevertheless,
we can't let things get out of hand," etc. etc.  This, however, has
the unfortunate effect of creating a false dichotomy between, on the
one hand, intrusive, destructive hackers, and on the other hand,
embattled, well-intentioned but occasionally slipshod sysadmins.

In fact the real discussion is whether curious seekers who roam the
net in increasing numbers will be viewed by the keepers of the net --
that is, to some degree or another, every participating institution --
as welcome guests or suspicious intruders.  And that will determine
whether the net keeps growing by leaps and bounds or goes flat some
day.

Non-technical types, like me, are coming to the net because we're
attracted by the wide variety of accessible information available at
sites around the world.  Since this net generally arrives at your
doorstep with a relatively small set of instructions, there is almost
from the get-go an implied tinkerer's attitude toward it.  To this
day news.announce.newusers is one of the top groups around and one
day probably will be far and away the number one group by readership.
If you have read Gene Spafford's postings or interacted with users
through mailing lists or help newsgroups, you know that there is an
esprit de corps (and how refreshing we can use that term in peacetime
to describe something other than the military!) about the net that is
infectious and useful.

In this evolving system of information, where curiosity is encouraged
and instructions are minimal, anonymous ftp is really a wonder.  Now,
I just said I was a non-techie, and really I am, I don't write programs
or even hacks.  But when I was in junior high I *did* earn the wrath
of the sysadmins for logging into other people's accounts in search
of adventure games.  (Remember those?  "You are standing at the end
of a road ...")  Had anonymous ftp existed then, I surely would not
have bothered to fish around until, quite by accident, I discovered
and easily cracked the system's password file.  (In retrospect, all
parties found my case to be hilarious and helpful.  But they sure
scared the shit out of me when I got caught.)

That's why I am very sensitive to charges that curious people are
hackers.  Even when *I* was "hacking," I really wasn't.  (We can split
that hair later, please, not now.)  And anyway, I was confronted
directly.  They did not even bother to inform my father, the chairman
of the physical sciences dept. at the time.  They went straight to me.
I'm glad they did.  In time, of course, my father found out, and my
account was revoked (his extra one, actually), but what if their story
had been all wrong?  There was a calm exercised in the course of that
prosecution that all evidence indicates was abandoned in the accusation
of Mr. Langford.

At any rate, that was 14 years ago.  I checked out of the computer scene
for many years, and have been on the net barely a year now.  I am
pretty impressed with what I see.  But in those intervening years I
have also seen my peers intimidated and discouraged from becoming
too curious or too outspoken by their own schools.  Newspapers harassed
for publishing politically incorrect views.  Groups pressured into
revoking their invitations to controversial speakers.  Doctoral
candidates deferring on dissertation topics of their choosing because
an advisor disliked it personally.  The latter example, of course, is
a perfect demonstration of where academic authority is legally
exercised at the expense of curiosity.  Like it or not, that's the 
convention nowadays.  (Tho' if PhD students are constantly playing
politics and not thinking, the institution also suffers.)

On the *net*, however, the convention is very clearly to encourage 
curiosity and minimize the role of authority.  The reasons, as our
discouraged friend Mr. Langston shows, are obvious.

So while I see some merit in giving SRI.COM a benefit of the doubt,
remember that they can jerk around a student, even one halfway 
across the country, pretty easily.  That has serious implications for
the future of the net, particularly in attracting info-seekers like
me who are bound to bump into walls in the course of our roamings.
What I don't like in this discussion is that each site is now free
to determine whether its anonymous ftp site is to have walls or not.
Isn't that what the password -- or better yet, telnet -- is for?

Of course, I am a technical rube, as has now been shamefully flogged
to the readership of comp.org.eff.talk and alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk.
But when technical smarts are considered the criterion for discussing
the net, you might as well shut down anonymous ftp right now, because
explorers like me obviously can't take any more subtle a hint.

Aaron

